.\" deviceterm(1) manual page.
.\" Groups and orders its COMMANDS to match the topic table in
.\" Sources/DeviceTermCLI/HelpCatalog.swift, which is what
.\" `deviceterm help` renders. Update both together when adding or
.\" renaming a verb; HelpCatalogTests guards the verb set, not the
.\" prose, so the wording here is maintained by hand.
.TH DEVICETERM 1 "2026-09-11" "deviceterm" "DeviceTerm Manual"
.SH NAME
deviceterm \- macOS terminal where Apple devices behave as first-class panes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B deviceterm
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.RB [ --pane
.IR ref ]
.RB [ --json ]
.PP
.B deviceterm help
.RI [ command | topic ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B deviceterm
is a macOS-native terminal application in which Apple-platform devices —
booted simulators (iOS, watchOS, iPadOS, tvOS) and physically-connected
iPhones/iPads — attach as first-class panes inside the tab. The
.B deviceterm
CLI runs inside a tab and drives the tab's device panes: touch,
hardware buttons, keyboard, rotation, the watchOS Digital Crown, and
accessibility inspection. Pass
.B --pane
.I ref
to disambiguate when the tab shows more than one device pane (resolving a
shortId, name, sim UDID, physical deviceId, or paneId).
.SH COMMANDS
Grouped below in the order
.B deviceterm help
lists them. Run
.B deviceterm help
.I command
to read that command's reference page, or
.B deviceterm agents
for the workflow and triage guide.
.SS Drive the device
Coords are normalized to what the device is showing; (0,0) top-left,
(1,1) bottom-right. A Simulator's observed rotation is followed; a
physical device starts in portrait and updates from DeviceTerm rotation
replies.
.TP
.B tap " " \fIx\fR " " \fIy\fR
Single discrete tap.
.TP
.B tap " " (\fB--identifier\fR \fIvalue\fR|\fB--label\fR \fIvalue\fR) " " [\fB--role\fR \fIvalue\fR] " " [\fB--value\fR \fIvalue\fR] " " [\fB--match\fR \fIexact\fR|\fIcontains\fR] " " [\fB--source\fR \fItree\fR|\fIsweep\fR] " " [\fB--step\fR \fI0..1\fR] " " [\fB--budget\fR \fIms\fR] " " [\fB--timeout\fR \fIms\fR]
Block until the accessibility selector resolves one eligible coordinate
target, then tap its centre. Takes the
.B wait ax
selector and makes the same selection
.B wait ax --print center
makes, so both act on the same element and no coordinate crosses the shell.
Refuses with
.B wait.unreachable
or
.B wait.ambiguous
rather than guess, and sends no tap when it does. A deadline after complete
observations with nothing matching is
.B wait.timeout
and exit 124, also with no tap; an incomplete observation reports its own
failure instead. The receipt adds the coordinate tapped, and the role when it
is a single word; under
.B --json
it adds role, label, identifier, matchCount, and elapsedMs. These flags require a
selector: a coordinate tap carrying one is a usage error.
.TP
.B swipe " " \fIfromX\fR " " \fIfromY\fR " " \fItoX\fR " " \fItoY\fR " " [\fB--duration\fR \fIms\fR] " " [\fB--hold\fR \fIms\fR]
Interpolated drag at ~60 Hz. Default 200 ms.
.B --hold
adds an active dwell at the end point (the finger is reported,
decelerated to a stop, before lifting).
.TP
.B long-press " " \fIx\fR " " \fIy\fR " " [\fB--duration\fR \fIms\fR]
Down at point, hold, up. Default 500 ms.
.TP
.B pinch " " \fIf1x f1y f2x f2y tf1x tf1y tf2x tf2y\fR " " [\fB--duration\fR \fIms\fR]
Two-finger interpolated path. Default 300 ms.
.TP
.B app-switcher
Open the iOS App Switcher with an edge-tagged swipe up from the bottom
edge with a dwell, a system gesture rather than a content swipe.
Confirmed in portrait and both landscape orientations; upside-down has no
edge value that arms the recognizer. A physical device that doesn't
support the edge gesture falls back to a consumer-HID Home double-press.
.SS Hardware input
.TP
.B button " " \fIhome\fR|\fIlock\fR|\fIside\fR|\fIapple-pay\fR|\fIsiri\fR|\fIdigital-crown\fR
Press a hardware button. Names are accepted in kebab-case (canonical),
snake_case, camelCase, or all lowercase. Use
.B crown
for watchOS rotation.
.TP
.B key " " \fIkeyCode\fR " " \fIdown\fR|\fIup\fR
kVK virtual key code (decimal or 0x-hex). Discrete events.
.TP
.B text " " \fIstring\fR
Type an ASCII string.
.TP
.B rotate " " \fIportrait\fR|\fIportrait-upside-down\fR|\fIlandscape-left\fR|\fIlandscape-right\fR|\fIleft\fR|\fIright\fR
Set device orientation and report success only after confirmation. Orientation names
match UIKit's UIDeviceOrientation, in the same spellings
.B button
accepts.
.I left
and
.I right
turn the device 90 degrees. A Simulator starts from its latest confirmed
display orientation; a physical device receives the relative operation
directly. A Simulator confirms through its observed display orientation, and a
physical device through the orientation returned by its control relay. Success
reports targetOrientation and observedOrientation. An orientation-locked
Simulator fails after the four-second confirmation deadline instead of
returning a dispatch receipt.
.TP
.B crown " " \fIdelta\fR " " [\fB--velocity\fR \fIv\fR] " " [\fB--duration\fR \fIms\fR]
Rotate the watchOS Digital Crown. Signed
.IR delta ;
positive is forward/down.
.B --velocity
is accepted on the wire but ignored at the daemon.
.SS Inspect
.TP
.B ax tree
Dump the frontmost iOS app's accessibility tree as JSON. Each usable node
includes
.B normalizedCenter
with x and y ready for a coordinate-bearing input verb. Its
.B frame
stays in displayed points for hit-target size checks. The response carries a
.B note
sentence and a
.B noteCode
token to branch on where the daemon has evidence the tree is short of the
screen. The walk returns empty by design on watchOS. Elsewhere the daemon
hit-tests the screen centre when no descendant covers it, and notes the tree
only when that point holds an element the tree lacks. One sample can prove an
omission and cannot rule one out, so an unnoted tree is not a tree known to be
complete. Both notes point at
.BR "ax sweep" .
.TP
.B ax point " " \fIx\fR " " \fIy\fR
Return one AX element at a normalized point. When present, the element's
.B normalizedCenter
uses the same displayed coordinate space and can be passed directly to
another coordinate-bearing verb. The field is omitted when the geometry
cannot produce an on-screen centre. The result also carries
.BR rootFrame ,
the screen the daemon measured, in displayed points. Multiply a normalized
centre by its width and height for point coordinates, without a second
.B ax tree
call. It is omitted when the preflight root carried no usable frame.
.TP
.B ax sweep " " [\fB--step\fR \fI0..1\fR] " " [\fB--budget\fR \fIms\fR]
Grid-walk the screen via objectAtPoint and aggregate unique elements.
Usable children include
.BR normalizedCenter .
The synthetic sweep root remains a 0,0,1,1 placeholder and has no
.BR normalizedCenter .
When the preflight yields a usable frame,
.B rootFrame
sits beside that placeholder and carries it. A sweep whose budget went before
the preflight ran reports none.
Default step 0.05, clamped silently into [0.02, 0.5]; the result echoes the
step used. A completed sweep makes ceil(1/step)^2 point queries, and a
control narrower than the step can fall between samples and read as absent.
.B --budget
bounds how long the daemon may spend scheduling those queries: default
10000, clamped into [0, 60000], echoed as budgetMs in the result. The 0.02
floor makes 2500 queries; whether they fit inside the default depends on the
host and the device, so read truncated rather than predicting it, and raise
the budget when it is set.
If the daemon finds its budget spent before the grid is done, it stops
before the next query, sets
.B truncated
in the result, and adds a
.B note
saying what to try: a larger --budget, or a coarser --step when the budget
was already at the ceiling.
.SS Wait for state
.TP
.B wait pane " " \fIbooting\fR|\fIrendering\fR|\fIshutdown\fR|\fIfailed\fR " " [\fB--timeout\fR \fIms\fR]
Block until the selected pane reaches the named lifecycle state.
.TP
.B wait ax " " (\fB--identifier\fR \fIvalue\fR|\fB--label\fR \fIvalue\fR) " " [\fB--role\fR \fIvalue\fR] " " [\fB--value\fR \fIvalue\fR] " " [\fB--match\fR \fIexact\fR|\fIcontains\fR] " " [\fB--state\fR \fIpresent\fR|\fIabsent\fR] " " [\fB--print\fR \fIcenter\fR] " " [\fB--source\fR \fItree\fR|\fIsweep\fR] " " [\fB--step\fR \fI0..1\fR] " " [\fB--budget\fR \fIms\fR] " " [\fB--timeout\fR \fIms\fR]
Block until the accessibility query matches, or matches nothing under
.BR "--state absent" ,
optionally with an exact role.
.B --match contains
matches a substring and folds case; the default is exact. Tree is the default
source. Step and budget apply only to sweep. The sweep budget is reduced to
the time remaining before the overall wait deadline.
.B --value
narrows to an element whose own value matches, under the same match mode.
A match answers a presence wait whatever the observation missed. Nothing
matching is a claim about everything it covered, so a wait that ends on an
observation which could not see the whole pane reports that, carrying the
daemon's note and noteCode, rather than a bare
.BR wait.timeout .
It reports on the probe that saw it when probing again cannot help, and at the
deadline when it can.
.B --state absent
waits for the query to match nothing and reports the condition
.BR ax.disappears .
Absence is a claim about what is not there, so an observation that did not
see everything cannot support it. A truncated sweep reports
.B wait.inconclusive
at once, and an unsupported walk reports
.BR wait.unsupported .
An incomplete tree is retried instead, and reports
.B wait.inconclusive
only if no complete observation arrives before the deadline. An element still
matching at the deadline is an ordinary
.BR wait.timeout .
It cannot be combined with
.BR "--print center" .
Under
.B --json
the receipt lists up to 20 matches with a matchCount for the true total and
matchesTruncated when the list was trimmed,
ranked presentational roles last, then entries carrying no normalizedCenter,
then smallest frame first. The ordering is a heuristic.
.B --print center
selects one eligible element and writes a bare x y, refusing with
wait.unreachable or wait.ambiguous rather than guess. It cannot be combined
with
.BR --json .
.TP
.B wait orientation " " \fIportrait\fR|\fIportrait-upside-down\fR|\fIlandscape-left\fR|\fIlandscape-right\fR " " [\fB--timeout\fR \fIms\fR]
Block until two consecutive observations report the requested confirmed
orientation with stable positive surface dimensions.
.TP
.B wait surface quiescent " " [\fB--settle\fR \fIms\fR] " " [\fB--timeout\fR \fIms\fR]
Block until the pane's rendered surface has stopped changing for
.B --settle
milliseconds, default 500. For a change with no element to wait for: a
Dynamic Type switch, a theme flip, an animation settling. Stillness is the
surface being unchanged rather than advanced by any amount, because a
Simulator bumps its sequence per frame while a device reports lease
generations that jump. A pane with no surface yet is pending rather than
quiescent; use
.B wait pane rendering
to wait for a first frame. This is not a rotation signal, since surface
dimensions do not swap when a device turns.
.PP
Every wait accepts
.B --pane
.IR ref .
The default timeout is 30000 ms. An overall deadline reports
.B wait.timeout
and exits 124. Unsupported observation, inconclusive coverage, and query
failures remain distinct. Each sub-verb reads its own flags beyond
.B --pane
and
.BR --timeout ,
and passing one to a wait that does not read it is a usage error rather than
a flag that gets ignored.
.SS Devices
.TP
.B devices list
The aggregate live roster: owned booted sims + connected physical devices,
each annotated with its attach/ownership state. Physical devices also
report model and OS version, disambiguating two devices that share a
name.
.TP
.B device attach " " \fIref\fR
The unified explicit-attach verb. Resolve
.I ref
against
.B devices list
(sim UDID, physical deviceId, or name) to claim a booted/orphan sim or
mirror a connected physical device into the caller's tab. A bare UDID
absent from the roster is claimed as an externally-booted sim.
This is the only explicit-attach verb; there is no
.BR "pane attach" .
.SS Manage the workspace
A tab is the workspace. It owns a split layout whose leaves are terminal,
Simulator, or physical-device panes. The public hierarchy uses singular
.BR window ,
.BR tab ,
and
.B pane
command roots.
.TP
.B window list " " [\fB--all\fR]
List the caller's window, or every caller-visible window with
.BR --all .
.TP
.B window show " " [\fIwindow\fR]
Show one window and its visible tabs.
.TP
.B window open
Requires a live automation grant. Open a window, first tab, and initial
terminal pane, returning all three committed objects.
.TP
.B window focus " " [\fIwindow\fR]
Requires a live automation grant. Raise the window and return committed focus
state.
.TP
.B window close " " [\fIwindow\fR] " " [\fB--mode\fR \fIdetach\fR|\fIshutdown\fR]
Close the window and its tabs. Detach leaves linked Simulators booted;
shutdown also shuts them down.
.TP
.B tab list " " [\fB--window\fR \fIwindow\fR|\fB--all\fR]
List tab workspaces, one row per GUI tab even when it has terminal splits.
.TP
.B tab show " " [\fItab\fR]
Show a tab, every pane in layout order, and the recursive split tree.
.TP
.B tab open " " [\fB--window\fR \fIwindow\fR] " " [\fB--cwd\fR \fIpath\fR] " " [\fB--command\fR \fIcmd\fR]
Requires a live automation grant. Wait for the tab and initial terminal
session to commit, then return the window, tab, and terminal pane. This waits
for the session ID, not shell readiness. A committed tab whose session mint
fails remains visible in failed state and is returned in the error details.
.TP
.B tab close " " [\fItab\fR] " " [\fB--mode\fR \fIdetach\fR|\fIshutdown\fR]
Close the tab. Closing a tab with other terminal sessions requires automation
authority.
.TP
.B tab rename " " [\fItab\fR] " " \fIname\fR
Assign or clear a stable tab name. One positional argument names the current
tab; two supply the tab ref and name. Quote a name containing spaces. More
than two positionals is a usage error. A word beginning with a dash is read
as a flag. Put
.B --
before the name to use it literally.
.TP
.B tab focus " " [\fItab\fR]
Requires a live automation grant. Select the tab and raise its window.
.TP
.B tab move " " [\fItab\fR] " " \fB--window\fR \fIwindow\fR " " [\fB--index\fR \fIn\fR]
Requires a live automation grant. Move the tab to the destination window,
appending unless a zero-based index is supplied.
.TP
.B tab protect " " [\fItab\fR]
Hide the tab and its panes from other sessions. Target-tab ownership or a live
automation grant is required.
.TP
.B tab unprotect " " [\fItab\fR]
Make the tab visible to other sessions. A grant never reveals a foreign
protected tab, so only an owning terminal can resolve that tab to unprotect it.
.TP
.B pane list " " [\fB--tab\fR \fItab\fR]
List terminal, Simulator, and physical-device panes in layout order.
.TP
.B pane show " " [\fIpane\fR]
Show one pane with its kind, capabilities, and kind-specific details.
.TP
.B pane split " " [\fIpane\fR] " " \fB--direction\fR \fIleft\fR|\fIright\fR|\fIup\fR|\fIdown\fR
Create a terminal beside the anchor, wait for its session ID, and return the
committed tab and pane.
.TP
.B pane focus " " [\fIpane\fR]
Requires a live automation grant. Raise and focus the pane.
.TP
.B pane close " " [\fIpane\fR] " " [\fB--mode\fR \fIdetach\fR|\fIshutdown\fR]
Close any pane kind. An explicit mode is valid only for a Simulator pane;
terminal and physical-device panes fail with
.BR intent.unsupportedPane .
Closing the last terminal fails with
.BR intent.wouldCloseTab ;
use
.B tab close
to remove the workspace.
.TP
.B pane rename " " [\fIpane\fR] " " \fIname\fR
Assign or clear a stable pane name. One positional argument names the current
pane; two supply the pane ref and name. Quote a name containing spaces. More
than two positionals is a usage error. A word beginning with a dash is read
as a flag. Put
.B --
before the name to use it literally.
.TP
.B pane send-input " " \fIpane\fR " " [\fB--type-delay\fR \fIms\fR] " " \fItext\fR
Requires a live automation grant and an explicit terminal pane. C-style
escapes are decoded. The receipt reports the committed pane and byte count,
never the text. A word beginning with a dash is read as a flag. Put
.B --
before the text to send such a word literally.
.TP
.B pane capture-text " " \fIpane\fR " " [\fB--ansi\fR]
Requires a live automation grant and an explicit terminal pane. Human mode
prints the visible viewport; JSON returns the pane and text. Scrollback is not
included.
.B --ansi
keeps the pane's SGR color and style sequences; palette colors stay as palette
indexes. Stripping those sequences and trimming trailing spaces on each row
yields the plain capture.
.TP
.B with-pane " " \fIref\fR " " \fIcmd\fR " " [\fIargs\fR ...]
Resolve a device pane, inject
.B DEVICETERM_TARGET_PANE
into the environment, and execute the child command.
.PP
Workspace refs resolve case-insensitively. Window and tab refs accept an exact
short ID, exact full UUID, exact unique name, or unique full-UUID prefix. Pane
refs accept an exact short ID, exact full ID, exact unique name, exact
Simulator UDID or physical device ID, or unique full-ID prefix. Names match
exactly, never by prefix. The one-based index printed by
.B window list
is display-order metadata, not a reference.
.PP
Window and tab short IDs are the first six lowercase hexadecimal characters
of their UUIDs. Pane short IDs are six lowercase Crockford base32 characters.
Omitted refs and
.B current
mean the object containing the calling terminal when the command permits
omission. A terminal pane's ID is its session ID.
.PP
For pane close and rename, an ungranted caller may target only its own terminal
session. Simulator and physical-device panes retain target-tab ownership. A
live automation grant satisfies either mutation check but never reveals a
foreign protected tab.
.PP
Workspace mutation receipts carry committed objects rather than request
echoes. Open and split receipts wait for terminal session creation, not shell
attachment. Device controls continue to accept
.B --pane
refs through their daemon-direct device roster.
.SS Setup and health
.TP
.B doctor
Check the pieces every other verb depends on: the session env vars,
whether
.B xcrun
resolves to the shim, the daemon socket and ping, and whether this
session is live and authenticates. Also reports the caller's identity,
its linked device panes, and the methods its transport and grant admit.
.B --json
emits the same report as an object. Exits 1 when any axis fails;
warnings alone still exit 0.
.TP
.B events
Subscribe to this session's event stream as one JSON object per line.
Long-running; exits on daemon EOF or Ctrl-C. The stream has no replay or
subscription-ready record. Use
.B wait
for one-shot convergence.
.TP
.B version
Print versions of deviceterm + daemon + RPC wire + macOS.
.TP
.B dump-config
Print every recognized
.I ~/.config/deviceterm/config
key with its current value and source layer.
.TP
.B completions install " " \fIzsh\fR|\fIbash\fR|\fIfish\fR
Generate and install the per-shell completion script. Prints the install
path and a one-line activation hint.
.SS Docs
.TP
.B help " " [\fIcommand\fR|\fItopic\fR]
Print the command list, or one command's reference page.
.B --help
and
.B -h
are the same verb. The concepts named at the foot of the list
(targeting, refs, output, troubleshooting) are addressable the same way.
.TP
.B agents
Print the workflow and triage guide: how a device pane attaches to a
tab, recipes for the common automation shapes, and the permission model
behind the errors the CLI returns.
.SH OUTPUT
Data commands support
.B --json
for machine-readable output. Successful lists remain arrays and successful
receipts remain their command-specific objects.
.PP
In JSON mode, every non-streaming invocation that fails after selecting JSON
emits one newline-terminated object on stdout:
.BR {"error":{"code":"stable.dottedCode","message":"diagnostic","details":{...}}} .
The command preserves its stderr diagnostic and nonzero exit status. Branch on
.B error.code
rather than stderr prose or exit 1. Optional
.B details
are command-specific and stable-additive.
.PP
Documentation commands
.RB ( --help ,
.BR agents )
remain prose. AX commands always emit JSON. The
.B events
command remains a JSON Lines stream rather than a single document.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DEVICETERM_SESSION
UUID identifying the caller's terminal session. Daemon-minted, injected by
the GUI when the terminal opens; each terminal split has its own session.
.TP
.B DEVICETERM_SESSION_CAP
Capability token for the session, validated on every session-scoped RPC.
.TP
.B DEVICETERM_DAEMON_SOCK
Path to the daemon's Unix-domain socket. Defaults to the per-user
location under Application Support.
.TP
.B DEVICETERM_SHIM_DIR
Per-session
.I bin/
holding the xcrun/simctl shim and the deviceterm-cli symlink.
.TP
.B DEVICETERM_TARGET_PANE
Optional device-pane target consumed by pane-targeted commands. Set by
.B deviceterm with-pane
(to the resolved pane's key) so child processes auto-target.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/deviceterm/config
User overrides. See
.B deviceterm dump-config
for recognized keys.
.TP
.I ~/Library/Caches/deviceterm/sessions/<sessionId>/
Per-session scratch directory: shim
.IR bin/ ,
the zsh
.IR ZDOTDIR ,
and the owner manifest.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Pane attachment is a precondition for input and AX commands. If
.B deviceterm tap
returns
.RI \(lq no device pane in this session\(rq ,
boot a sim from this terminal with
.B xcrun simctl boot \fIUDID\fR
\(em the deviceterm shim intercepts the boot and creates the pane. Custom
helpers that bypass
.B xcrun simctl boot
won't create a pane. Run
.B deviceterm doctor
for an env + daemon + session health check, or
.B deviceterm agents
for the full triage guide.
.PP
Workspace failures use stable
.B intent.*
codes. In particular,
.B intent.wouldCloseTab
requires an explicit
.BR "tab close" ;
.B intent.unsupportedPane
means the resolved pane kind does not implement that operation; and
.B intent.mutationFailed
means an earlier part of a compound mutation committed. In JSON mode, retain
.B error.details.committed
so the committed object remains addressable.
